## Visual and Audio Systems

### Sound System
- Asset paths
  - Base SFX: `public/assets/sounds/{explosion.mp3,jump.mp3}`
  - Fanfares: `public/assets/sounds/fanfares/sfx_sounds_fanfare3.wav`
  - Positive: `public/assets/sounds/positive/sfx_sounds_powerup4.wav`
  - 8‑bit collection (categories): `public/assets/sounds/8bit/The Essential Retro Video Game Sound Effects Collection [512 sounds] By Juhani Junkala/`
    - Includes subfolders like Explosions, General Sounds (Menu, Positive/Negative, Coins, Damage), Movement (Jumping and Landing, Footsteps), Weapons, Death Screams, etc.
    - Pruned: only `General Sounds/Simple Damage Sounds/sfx_damage_hit5.wav` is retained from the 8-bit library; unused files removed to reduce size.

- Implementation (HTML5 Audio)
  - Audio elements are declared in `public/index.html` with IDs and `preload="auto"`:
    - `fight-sound`, `jump-sound`, `death-sound`, `round-end-sound`, `score-sound`
  - Playback is triggered from `public/app.js` by selecting the element and calling `play()`; volume and `currentTime` are set when used.
    - Fight: on FIGHT! message show
    - Round end: when round over UI shows
    - Score: when a player's kill count increases
  - Currently wired: `fight-sound`, `round-end-sound`, `score-sound`, `jump-sound` (on local jump input), and `death-sound` (global with 800ms cooldown; short 8-bit: General Sounds/Simple Damage Sounds/sfx_damage_hit5.wav).
- Approach
  - Uses HTML5 `Audio` elements (no Web Audio API pipeline or `THREE.Audio` yet). This keeps implementation simple and avoids CORS/decoder complexity.
  - If positional/spatial audio is desired later, `THREE.Audio`/`PositionalAudio` and `THREE.AudioLoader` are available via Three.js 0.175.0.
- Example usages
  - Fight sound (app.js): get element by ID and `play()`
  - Round end sound (app.js): volume set to 0.5, `currentTime=0` before play
  - Score sound (app.js): triggered when kills increase

### Minecraft Skins Integration
- Files
  - Client fetcher: `public/MinecraftSkinFetcher.js`
  - Client model application: `public/Guy.js` (`applyMinecraftSkin`)
  - Server proxy endpoints: `server.js`
- Flow
  1. Client requests skin URL by username via `MinecraftSkinFetcher.getSkinUrl(username)`.
  2. Server proxies Mojang APIs to avoid CORS and rate‑limit issues:
     - `GET /api/minecraft/user/:username` → `https://api.mojang.com/users/profiles/minecraft/{username}` to resolve UUID
     - `GET /api/minecraft/profile/:userId` → `https://sessionserver.mojang.com/session/minecraft/profile/{uuid}` to get `properties["textures"].value` (base64 JSON) and extract `textures.SKIN.url`
  3. Client caches results per username (`skinCache`).
  4. In `public/app.js`, when an avatar with a name appears or the name changes, it fetches the skin URL and applies it.
  5. Application: `Guy.applyMinecraftSkin(skinUrl)` loads the texture with `THREE.TextureLoader`, disables `flipY`, and assigns a `MeshStandardMaterial` with the skin texture to head/body/limbs. Sets `hasMinecraftSkin=true` so later color updates do not override the skin.

- Caching and fallback
  - Client‑side caches: username→skinUrl (`MinecraftSkinFetcher`).
  - On error/no skin: returns `null`; code logs a warning and leaves default materials/colors in place (no hard fallback texture).
- Limitations
  - No handling of layered hat/outerwear, slim (Alex) vs classic (Steve) arm geometry, or capes.
  - No persistent server cache; requests are proxied live to Mojang.
  - Skin mapping currently applies one material to all parts in `Guy.applyMinecraftSkin` (simple mapping).
- Interesting usernames to test
  - notch, jeb_, Dinnerbone, Grumm, DanTDM, Dream, Grian, MumboJumbo, Captainsparklez, Technoblade

### Graphics, Textures, and Visual Effects
- Texture systems in use
  - File textures via `THREE.TextureLoader`:
    - Main platform: `public/assets/textures/platform-main.jpg`
    - Death platform: `public/assets/textures/platform-death.jpg`
    - Clouds: `public/assets/images/{cloud1.png, cloud2.png}`
  - Procedural/Canvas textures:
    - Player name labels and the temporary "JUMP!" indicator use `THREE.CanvasTexture` with `THREE.SpriteMaterial` and depth disabled to always render on top.
- Materials
  - Avatars: `THREE.MeshStandardMaterial` with `color`, `emissive`, `metalness`, `roughness` (see `public/Guy.js`). When a Minecraft skin is applied, a textured `MeshStandardMaterial` replaces part materials.
  - Platforms: `THREE.MeshStandardMaterial` with emissive “glow” color; auxiliary `THREE.MeshBasicMaterial` boxes are added as translucent, slightly larger shells to fake a glow outline; `THREE.EdgesGeometry` with `THREE.LineBasicMaterial` adds edge highlights.
  - Sprites: `THREE.SpriteMaterial` for UI name labels and clouds.
- Particles and effects
  - Explosion on death/respawn: instanced small `SphereGeometry` meshes with `MeshBasicMaterial`, animated with gravity/fade/scale down; cleaned up after life ends.
  - Death platform "lava" shimmer: group of small `SphereGeometry` meshes animated with sine waves and opacity pulsing.
  - Movement trails: short‑lived particle groups spawned on double‑tap run; downward crush action spawns a one‑shot vertical trail.
  - Background: large dark plane + multiple cloud sprites drifting horizontally, plus star meshes with twinkle animation.
  - UI effects: CSS keyframe explosion animation applied to the on‑screen “FIGHT!” message; scoreboard and messages use a retro bitmap font via Google Fonts CDN.
- Post‑processing
  - Three.js example scripts for post‑processing and UnrealBloom are included in `index.html`, but the scene currently renders directly without an `EffectComposer`. Bloom can be enabled later if desired.

